{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
<p>Kiln is a template language for building websites on the server. You write HTML with a small set of tags and expressions, and Kiln turns it into a page.</p>

{% apply example(context: { site: { name: "Acme" }, posts: [{ title: "Hello, world", url: "/blog/hello/" }, { title: "Our first year", url: "/blog/year-one/" }] }) %}
{% verbatim %}
<h1>{{ site.name|upper }}</h1>
<ul>
	{% for post in posts %}
		<li><a href="{{ post.url }}">{{ post.title }}</a></li>
	{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

<p>If you have used Twig, Jinja or Liquid, this will look familiar. Kiln keeps the syntax people and AI models already know, and changes the parts that tend to cause bugs.</p>

<h2>What Kiln is good at</h2>

<ul>
	<li><strong>Server-rendered sites.</strong> Layouts, components with typed props, includes and macros cover what a website needs.</li>
	<li><strong>Loading data from templates.</strong> Templates can call async functions, such as <code>document("page")</code>. Kiln only waits when a call returns a promise.</li>
	<li><strong>Running anywhere.</strong> The core has no dependencies and never uses <code>eval</code>, so it runs on Node and Cloudflare Workers.</li>
	<li><strong>Templates you didn't write.</strong> Templates can only read data you pass them. Output is escaped by default, and limits stop templates that run too long.</li>
	<li><strong>Clear errors.</strong> Strict mode catches typos, and every error shows the template, line and column.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How it works</h2>

<p>You create an environment once, with a loader that finds your templates and any functions or filters you want to add. Then you render templates by name.</p>

{% apply highlight("ts", title: "server.ts") %}
{% verbatim %}
import { createKiln } from "@lucidcms/kiln";
import { fileLoader } from "@lucidcms/kiln/node";

const kiln = createKiln({
	loader: fileLoader({ root: "templates" }),
});

const html = await kiln.render("pages/home", { title: "Welcome" });
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

<p>Kiln compiles each template once and keeps it in memory, so later renders are fast.</p>

<h2>Where to go next</h2>

<ul>
	<li><a href="/docs/installation/">Install Kiln</a> in a Node or Workers project.</li>
	<li>Follow the <a href="/docs/quick-start/">quick start</a> to render a page with a layout.</li>
	<li>Read <a href="/docs/coming-from-twig/">Coming from Twig</a> if you know Twig already.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}
